    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `summarize_ticket` in `app/summary.py` and added tests for it. All 4 tests pass.\n\n```python\nmessages = [SystemMessage(SUMMARY_PROMPT), HumanMessage(ticket_text(ticket))]\nreply = chat.invoke(messages, stop=[\"\\n\\n\"])\nreturn str(reply.text).strip()\n```\n\n- **One request:** it makes a single `chat.invoke` call with the system message and the user message.\n- **Stop sequence:** it passes `stop=[\"\\n\\n\"]` through the standard `invoke` argument, so any LangChain chat model will stop at the first blank line.\n- **Plain str:** it reads `reply.text` rather than `str(reply.content)`. Some chat models (Anthropic, for example) return their content as a list of blocks, and `str(content)` would turn that into a Python list repr instead of the reply text. `.text` joins the text blocks, and the `str(...)` converts the result to a plain `str`.\n\n**Tests:** the new `tests/test_summary.py` uses a fake chat model that records its calls. The tests check that there is exactly one call, the stop list is `[\"\\n\\n\"]`, the two messages have the right types and content, whitespace is stripped, and replies made of content blocks are handled.\n\nI couldn't use Context7 because its monthly quota is exceeded. Instead I checked the installed `langchain-core` 1.6.5 source directly to confirm that `invoke` accepts `stop` and that `.text` is a property. No dependencies were changed.\n\n`app/translate.py` still uses `str(message.content)`, so it would give the wrong text with block-content models. I left it alone because it's outside this task."
  },
